-- Lesson enrichment, November 9, 2026, part 2: English 4 and World History 1.
-- Adds a "Worked example" and "Common mistake" paragraph before "Your task:" in each of the 48 lessons.
-- Safe to run more than once (skips lessons that already contain "Worked example:").
-- Each statement keys on course slug and lesson position.

-- ENGLISH 4
with e(pos, txt) as (values
(1, $e$Worked example: a kenning is a compact poetic name made of two ideas, such as "whale road" for the sea. Alliteration repeats opening sounds, as in the phrase mighty monster of the mere. When you read an epic, mark each of these devices and ask what it adds. A kenning makes the reader picture the sea in a new way, and alliteration gives the line a strong beat when spoken aloud. Common mistake: reading Beowulf as only action. The poem also teaches values, such as loyalty, courage and the danger of pride, so always ask what the poem praises.$e$),
(2, $e$Worked example: read the opening aloud, "Whan that Aprill with his shoures soote," and you can guess that shoures means showers and soote means sweet. Chaucer lets each pilgrim reveal character through details. The Prioress is polite and tender hearted, yet her careful table manners and fine clothing suggest vanity more than humility. A strong answer names the detail, then states what it shows. Common mistake: assuming Chaucer praises every pilgrim. His gentle satire exposes flaws as well as virtues, so read each portrait for both.$e$),
(3, $e$Worked example: when Goods refuses to go with Everyman, the name does the teaching. Goods stands for wealth, and its refusal shows that riches cannot follow us past death. Good Deeds is weak but faithful, which shows that a neglected life of service leaves little to offer at the end. Compare 1 Timothy 6:7, "For we brought nothing into this world, and it is certain we can carry nothing out." Common mistake: treating the characters as ordinary people. In an allegory each one is an idea, so ask what it stands for.$e$),
(4, $e$Worked example: trace the history inside a few words. The animals kept their English names in the field, cow and pig, but their meat took French names at the table, beef and pork, because French speaking nobles ate it. The phrase "thou shalt" in the King James Bible shows Early Modern English, where thou meant you when speaking to one person. Common mistake: thinking Old English is just misspelled modern English. It was a separate Germanic language, and a modern reader cannot read it without study.$e$),
(5, $e$Worked example: say "Shall I compare thee to a summer's day?" aloud and tap the beat. It has ten syllables, and the strongest beats fall on pare, sum and day, which is the da DUM pattern of iambic pentameter. In this sonnet the volta, the turn in thought, comes at line 9, "But thy eternal summer shall not fade," where the poet moves from the weakness of summer to the lasting power of his poem. Common mistake: looking for the turn only in the last two lines. It often begins at line 9, and the couplet then draws the conclusion.$e$),
(6, $e$Worked example: use a four part frame. Flaw: in Macbeth the flaw is ambition. Choice: he decides to murder King Duncan. Fall: guilt and fear drive him to more killing, until he is defeated. Meaning: ambition without conscience destroys the one who pursues it. Write one sentence for each part and your analysis is complete. Common mistake: blaming fate or the witches alone. The witches only predict, while Macbeth chooses his actions, and tragedy depends on that choice.$e$),
(7, $e$Worked example: read Proverbs 15:1 aloud, "A soft answer turneth away wrath: but grievous words stir up anger." The two halves balance each other and set opposites side by side, which is parallelism. The rhythm is easy to speak and easy to remember, which is why such lines entered everyday English. To analyze a verse, name the device, quote the words and explain the effect. Common mistake: studying only what the verse says. Read it aloud and notice how it sounds, because the translators wrote for the ear.$e$),
(8, $e$Worked example: in "Death, be not proud," Donne calls death a slave to "Fate, Chance, kings, and desperate men," which strips death of its power. The conceit treats death as a defeated enemy, and the closing paradox, "Death, thou shalt die," says death itself will end. Compare 1 Corinthians 15:26, "The last enemy that shall be destroyed is death." Common mistake: skipping a paradox because it seems impossible. Ask in what sense it is true, and the poem's faith becomes clear.$e$),
(9, $e$Worked example: in "A Modest Proposal," published in 1729, Swift calmly suggests that poor Irish families sell their children as food. He does not mean it. His real target is the cold indifference of the wealthy toward the suffering of the poor. The calm tone makes the horror stronger, which is irony at work. To analyze satire, name the target, the technique and the intended change. Common mistake: taking satire literally. Ask what is being criticized and how the author exaggerates it.$e$),
(10, $e$Worked example: in "To err is human, to forgive divine," the two halves balance, human against divine, and the line is short enough to remember. That compact balance is why Pope's lines became sayings. Johnson's wit shows in his definition of oats: "A grain, which in England is generally given to horses, but in Scotland supports the people." Common mistake: thinking a dictionary is only a list of meanings. Johnson used quotations to show how words were actually used, and some definitions carry his personality.$e$),
(11, $e$Worked example: match each name to its meaning. The Slough of Despond stands for discouragement that swallows a believer. Vanity Fair stands for the temptation of worldly show and pleasure. The Celestial City stands for heaven. Compare Hebrews 11:13, "and confessed that they were strangers and pilgrims on the earth." Common mistake: reading the book as only a travel story. Every place and person has a second meaning, so ask what the name tells you about the Christian life.$e$),
(12, $e$Worked example: "You cannot trust his budget plan, because he failed a math class years ago." That is an ad hominem, since it attacks the person and ignores the plan. A fair response examines the numbers. Another: "Either we ban phones completely, or students will never learn." That is a false dilemma, because there are more choices, such as limits during class. Common mistake: calling an argument a fallacy only because you dislike its conclusion. Name the exact flaw in the reasoning, and show how to repair it.$e$),
(13, $e$Worked example: Blake asks in "The Tyger," "What immortal hand or eye could frame thy fearful symmetry?" The question marvels at a Creator who made both beauty and danger. Wordsworth follows a different pattern: an experience in nature, then reflection on its lasting effect, as when the daffodils return to him in "the inward eye which is the bliss of solitude." Common mistake: summarizing the scene and stopping. Always explain what the experience does to the speaker and what the poem says about nature or the soul.$e$),
(14, $e$Worked example: the creature in the novel has no name. Frankenstein is the name of the scientist, Victor, who abandons what he made. The creature learns language, longs for friendship and turns bitter only after being rejected. That sequence raises Shelley's question: who is responsible for what a creator brings into the world? Common mistake: calling the creature Frankenstein and treating the book as only a horror story. Notice who acts monstrously and who shows suffering, and form your own supported answer.$e$),
(15, $e$Worked example: in A Christmas Carol, Scrooge begins by saying "Bah! Humbug!" and ends by keeping Christmas well. The change comes through three spirits, each showing him something he has refused to see: his past, the present need around him and a lonely future. Dickens uses the change to argue that wealth carries a duty to others. Common mistake: reading Dickens only as entertainment. Each story has a target in society, such as the workhouse or the debtors' prison, so ask what injustice he exposes.$e$),
(16, $e$Worked example: Tennyson's Ulysses ends with a resolve "To strive, to seek, to find, and not to yield." The series of verbs shows determination in the rhythm itself. Browning's duke opens with "That's my last Duchess painted on the wall," and without meaning to he reveals his pride and cruelty as he speaks. The reader sees more than the speaker does. Common mistake: trusting the speaker of a dramatic monologue. Ask what the speaker reveals that he does not intend, and quote the line that shows it.$e$),
(17, $e$Worked example: The Waste Land opens, "April is the cruellest month." Compare Chaucer, whose April brings showers and the hope of pilgrimage. Eliot reverses that renewal, which is an allusion, and the reversal shows how broken the world feels after the war. A reader who knows Chaucer hears the contrast. Common mistake: expecting a clear plot from a Modernist poem. Look for fragments, voices and allusions, and ask how they fit together to show a fractured world.$e$),
(18, $e$Worked example: Owen describes the soldiers: "Bent double, like old beggars under sacks." The simile strips away any picture of glory and shows young men aged by exhaustion. The poem then ends by calling the old saying that it is sweet and fitting to die for one's country "the old Lie." To analyze it, quote the image, name the device and state the argument. Common mistake: saying only that the poem is sad. Name what it argues, which is that the glory of war is a lie to those who have seen it.$e$),
(19, $e$Worked example: in Animal Farm the original commandments are changed until one remains: "All animals are equal but some animals are more equal than others." The bent sentence shows how those in power twist language to hide injustice. Lewis teaches by a similar reversal in The Screwtape Letters, where the reader learns about temptation by reading the tempter's advice. Common mistake: treating an allegory as only about its own time. Ask what remains true today and where you see it.$e$),
(20, $e$Worked example: Achebe writes in Things Fall Apart, "Proverbs are the palm oil with which words are eaten." The line shows how central proverbs are to Igbo speech and how a culture describes its own way of speaking. Achebe shows village life from the inside, with its justice, festivals and customs, before the arrival of the colonial order. Common mistake: judging the village through an outsider's eyes. Notice who tells the story and from what point of view, and how the book answers earlier stories that portrayed Africa as simple.$e$),
(21, $e$Worked example: suppose your question is how sleep affects school performance. A weak paragraph summarizes Source A, then Source B. A synthesis paragraph joins them: "While one study finds that students who sleep eight hours score higher, a district report adds that later start times improved attendance; together, these sources suggest that rest supports both learning and attendance." Common mistake: arranging your essay source by source. Organize by idea, let sources speak to each other, and cite each one.$e$),
(22, $e$Worked example: a weak opening says, "Leadership is important to me." A strong opening starts in a scene: "At six in the morning I turned the key in the church kitchen door." The reader is placed in a moment, and the meaning can then widen to what that service taught about humility. Common mistake: turning the essay into a list of activities. Choose one moment, show it with details, and spend the final paragraph on what you learned and how you changed.$e$),
(23, $e$Worked example: compare two subject lines. Weak: "Hi." Strong: "Application for Youth Assistant Position, Maria Lopez." The second tells the reader exactly why you are writing. Do the same for a resume line. Weak: "Was in charge of volunteers." Strong: "Led a team of eight volunteers to serve 120 breakfasts each month." Common mistake: listing duties instead of results. Begin each line with an action verb and add a number or outcome whenever you can.$e$),
(24, $e$Worked example: an opening might be, "Raise your hand if someone has ever told you to speak up." It gets attention and leads to your main point. Then give two or three main ideas, each with one example, and end by restating your point with a final thought or a call to action. Practice aloud with a timer. Common mistake: reading your slides word for word and speaking too fast. Slow down, pause after key points and look at your audience.$e$))
update course_items i set content = jsonb_set(i.content,'{body}', to_jsonb(replace(i.content->>'body', 'Your task:', e.txt || E'\n\nYour task:'))) from e, courses c where c.id=i.course_id and c.slug='english-4' and i.kind='topic' and i.position=e.pos and i.content->>'body' not like '%Worked example:%';

-- WORLD HISTORY 1
with e(pos, txt) as (values
(1, $e$Worked example: place three events on a timeline: Constantinople dedicated in 330, the fall of the Western Roman Empire in 476, and the Hijra in 622. The first comes earliest, and 146 years separate 330 from 476. The year 476 belongs to the 400s, the fifth century, and 622 to the seventh. Common mistake: counting centuries one off. Years 1 to 100 are the first century, so the 1500s are the sixteenth century. For cause and effect, write what happened, why it happened and what followed.$e$),
(2, $e$Worked example: a soldier's letter written in 1916 is a primary source for World War I. A textbook chapter written today is a secondary source. The same item can be both: a biography of Constantine written in 1950 is secondary for Constantine but primary for how people in 1950 thought. Ask who made the source, why and for whom. Common mistake: believing a primary source is always true. Authors have viewpoints, so compare the source with others before you trust it.$e$),
(3, $e$Worked example: historians ask why Rome fell. One stresses invasions by Germanic peoples. Another stresses economic trouble and weak government. A strong answer states both views, gives evidence for each, then explains which one the evidence supports better and why. Common mistake: choosing a single cause because it sounds simple, or saying that everyone has an opinion so no view is better. Evidence decides, so always quote or name the evidence behind your conclusion.$e$),
(4, $e$Worked example: a town meeting where citizens speak and vote echoes Athens. A written law code that applies to everyone echoes Rome. The belief in one God and a moral law echoes Israel. Make a three column chart with a gift from each and an example from today. Common mistake: crediting only one stream for the West. Each contributed something different, and the three together help explain how Western civilization took shape.$e$),
(5, $e$Worked example: follow a cause and effect chain. Constantinople sat on the Bosporus, where trade passed between the Black Sea and the Mediterranean and between Europe and Asia. That position brought wealth and made the city easy to defend. As a result, the empire outlived Rome in the West by nearly a thousand years. Justinian's Code kept Roman law alive, and Hagia Sophia, built in the 530s, shows the empire's skill. Common mistake: saying that Rome ended completely in 476. The Eastern half continued.$e$),
(6, $e$Worked example: put three events in order. The Iconoclast controversy began in the 700s over the use of icons. The Great Schism of 1054 divided the Church in the East from the Church in the West over matters such as the pope's authority. In 1453 the Ottoman Turks, under Mehmed II, took Constantinople. Common mistake: treating the Schism as one argument on one day. Tensions grew for centuries, and 1054 marks the break. Write one sentence on cause and one on effect for each event.$e$),
(7, $e$Worked example: build a chart of three faiths. Judaism, Christianity and Islam each teach belief in one God, and each traces its roots to Abraham. They differ on who Jesus is. Christians confess Jesus as the Son of God and Savior, Muslims honor Jesus as a prophet, and Jews do not accept him as the Messiah. Common mistake: describing a faith unfairly. Describe each faith the way its own followers would, then state the differences plainly and respectfully.$e$),
(8, $e$Worked example: in Baghdad, scholars in the House of Wisdom translated Greek works into Arabic and added their own discoveries. Al Khwarizmi wrote a book on solving equations whose title gave us the word algebra, and Hindu Arabic numerals reached Europe through Muslim lands. Common mistake: saying that Muslim scholars only copied older works. They made new advances in mathematics, medicine and astronomy. When you write, name a scholar, the field and the contribution.$e$),
(9, $e$Worked example: draw the pyramid of feudalism. The king grants land to great lords, called vassals, who promise military service. They grant land to knights, and peasants and serfs work the manor in return for protection. Write one sentence: "A serf worked the lord's land in exchange for protection and a place to live." Common mistake: calling serfs enslaved people. Serfs were tied to the land and owed labor, but a lord could not usually sell a serf apart from the land. Explain the difference.$e$),
(10, $e$Worked example: in 1215 the barons forced King John to agree to Magna Carta at Runnymede. It said the king himself must obey the law, an idea that later shaped English government. Meanwhile, trade fairs, guilds and merchants helped towns grow, and towns gave people new ways to earn a living. Common mistake: saying Magna Carta gave rights to everyone in 1215. At first it protected mostly the barons, but the principle that the king is under law grew over time.$e$),
(11, $e$Worked example: sort the Crusades into a cause and effect chart. Causes: the Byzantine appeal for help, pilgrimage to the Holy Land and the hope of Church reform. Effects: more trade with the East, a weakened Byzantium after the sack of 1204, lasting bitterness between Christians and Muslims, and attacks on Jewish communities. The Black Death of 1347 to 1351 later killed perhaps one third of Europe. Common mistake: listing only battles. Always add the lasting effects on trade, politics and people.$e$),
(12, $e$Worked example: compare Japan with Europe. A shogun held real power, as a strong king might, while the emperor remained a symbol. A daimyo was a regional lord, like a great vassal, and a samurai served him, like a knight. The samurai followed a code of loyalty and honor called bushido. In 1192 Minamoto Yoritomo became shogun. Common mistake: thinking the emperor ruled Japan. Explain who held power and who held honor.$e$),
(13, $e$Worked example: each region had what the other lacked. The Sahara had salt, and the south had gold, so caravans carried both and traders became rich. In 1324 Mansa Musa of Mali made a pilgrimage to Mecca and gave away so much gold that prices in Cairo fell. Timbuktu grew into a center of learning. Common mistake: mixing up the order of empires. Remember Ghana first, Mali second and Songhai third, and attach one fact to each.$e$),
(14, $e$Worked example: match each place to its key fact. Lalibela is known for churches carved from solid rock in Ethiopia. Kilwa was a city state on the Swahili Coast that traded gold. Great Zimbabwe is known for its stone walls. In Kongo, King Afonso I became Christian and wrote to the king of Portugal to protest the slave trade. Common mistake: treating Africa as one place. Name the region, the people and the evidence every time.$e$),
(15, $e$Worked example: the Maya built cities such as Tikal, wrote in glyphs, tracked time with a calendar called the Long Count and used the idea of zero. Compare the Olmec, who carved huge stone heads and traded widely. Common mistake: saying the Maya vanished. Their classic cities declined around the year 900, but Maya people live in Mexico and Guatemala today. When you compare civilizations, give one achievement, one location and one date for each.$e$),
(16, $e$Worked example: contrast the two empires. The Aztec ruled by demanding tribute from conquered peoples. The Inca ruled through a network of roads and used khipu, knotted cords, to keep records, with their capital at Cuzco. Cortes reached the Aztec in 1519 and Pizarro met the Inca in 1532. Common mistake: crediting the Spanish conquests to weapons alone. Smallpox and the help of native allies who resented the Aztec also changed the outcome. List all three factors.$e$),
(17, $e$Worked example: compare a medieval painting, where figures often look flat, with a Renaissance painting that uses perspective to show depth. Leonardo da Vinci painted The Last Supper, and Michelangelo painted the Sistine Chapel ceiling between 1508 and 1512. Around 1450 Johannes Gutenberg's printing press began printing books, including the Bible, which spread ideas quickly. Common mistake: saying the Renaissance rejected faith. Many artists painted scenes from Scripture, and humanism grew alongside belief.$e$),
(18, $e$Worked example: follow the chain. Indulgences were sold, Luther posted the Ninety Five Theses in 1517, the printing press spread them quickly, and in 1521 Luther refused to take back his words at the Diet of Worms. Protestant churches formed. The Catholic Reformation answered with the Council of Trent and the Jesuits. Common mistake: saying Luther set out to start a new church. He first called for reform, and the break came as the dispute grew. Explain this in your own words.$e$),
(19, $e$Worked example: in 1610 Galileo used a telescope to see moons around Jupiter, which showed that not everything orbits the Earth. He followed a method: ask a question, make a hypothesis, test it, and draw a conclusion from the evidence. In 1687 Isaac Newton published his laws of motion and gravity. Common mistake: describing the period as science against faith. Kepler and Newton believed they were studying the order God created. Use a quotation or example to show it.$e$),
(20, $e$Worked example: sort the Columbian Exchange. From the Americas to Europe went potatoes, corn, tomatoes and tobacco. From Europe to the Americas went horses, wheat, sugarcane and diseases such as smallpox. Write one sentence on what the exchange gave and one on what it cost. Common mistake: describing the exchange as only a benefit. Disease killed millions of native people, and the slave trade forced millions of Africans across the Atlantic. A full answer weighs both sides.$e$),
(21, $e$Worked example: match each thinker to one idea. John Locke argued that people have natural rights to life, liberty and property. Montesquieu argued for separating the powers of government. Rousseau wrote about the social contract. These ideas shaped constitutions and declarations of rights, including the American Declaration of Independence. Common mistake: mixing up Locke and Montesquieu. Remember that Locke is about rights and Montesquieu is about separated powers, and attach each to an example.$e$),
(22, $e$Worked example: build a timeline. 1789: the Bastille falls. 1793: Louis XVI is executed. 1793 and 1794: the Reign of Terror. 1799: Napoleon takes power. 1804: he becomes emperor. 1815: he is defeated at Waterloo. In 1804 Haiti declared independence after an uprising of enslaved people, and later Simon Bolivar led independence movements in South America. Common mistake: assuming every revolution ends in freedom. In France it led to terror and then a dictator.$e$),
(23, $e$Worked example: write a chain. Coal and iron plus the steam engine produced factories. Railroads moved goods. Cities grew fast, and children worked long hours, which led to reform laws. Adam Smith argued for free markets, while Karl Marx argued for socialism. Common mistake: calling the Industrial Revolution only good or only bad. It raised production and living standards over time, and it brought crowded cities and hard labor. Give one example of each.$e$),
(24, $e$Worked example: put these in order and say what each led to. 1914: World War I begins. 1919: the Treaty of Versailles. 1939: World War II begins. 1945: the war ends. 1947: the Cold War takes shape. 1989: the Berlin Wall falls. 1991: the Soviet Union dissolves. Approach the Holocaust with care and respect: about six million Jews were murdered. Common mistake: memorizing dates without connections. Explain how each event helped cause the next.$e$))
